+### The notes kinds
+
+`release.notes.kind` names where a release's notes live. Every kind answers the same questions about a version -- is its note open or dated, what its body is, which paths a release of it owns -- so nothing else in the core reads a note.
+
+| Kind        | A version's note                                                         | Open while                                  | Dated when                                | Generated views                  |
+| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
+| `releases`  | `RELEASES/<version>.md`: a `key: value` header, then the body            | `status: unreleased`, with no `date:`       | `status: released` and `date: YYYY-MM-DD` | `CHANGELOG.md` and `RELEASES.md` |
+| `changelog` | the `## [<version>] - <marker>` section of a hand-written `CHANGELOG.md` | the marker is `unreleased` or `in progress` | the marker is `YYYY-MM-DD`                | none                             |
+| `command`   | whatever `release.notes.run` answers                                     | its `state` prints `open`                   | its `state` prints `dated YYYY-MM-DD`     | whatever its `views` writes      |
+
+**`releases`** renders `CHANGELOG.md` from each note's `## Added` to `## Security` sections and `RELEASES.md` as the index, and `release check` compares both with a fresh render without writing. Only a file named `X.Y.Z.md` is a note: any other `.md` under `RELEASES/` is refused by name. A released note is never re-dated.
+
+**`changelog`** reads only the heading of the version asked about, so a history of free-text headings is never parsed. A heading for that version with no marker, a marker that is none of the three, or a second heading for it is refused. The body is the section up to the next `## ` heading, without its leading and trailing blank lines.
+
+**`command`** runs `release.notes.run` at the project root with a verb, split into words and never `eval`'d. `state <version>` prints `absent`, `open` or `dated YYYY-MM-DD`; `date <version> <YYYY-MM-DD>` dates the note and prints each path it wrote; `body <version>` prints the body; `views` regenerates any generated views and prints each path; `views-check` prints `current <path>` or `STALE <path>` for each view and writes nothing; `paths <version>` prints every path a release of that version owns. Paths are relative to the project root, and an answer in any other shape is refused, naming what was printed.
 
